{"alignments": [{"id": "ISA.15.8.a001", "note": "The conjunction is rendered “For.”", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.001"], "source_head": "WLC.ISA.15.8.001", "status": "aligned", "target": ["t001"], "target_head": "t001"}, {"id": "ISA.15.8.a002", "note": "The encircling verb is rendered by “is gone round about.” I take “round about” as its fixed complement, headed on the lexeme-bearing “round”; the competing analysis assigns only “about” to the object marker as an object-linking preposition.", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.002"], "source_head": "WLC.ISA.15.8.002", "status": "aligned", "target": ["t004", "t005", "t006", "t007"], "target_head": "t006"}, {"id": "ISA.15.8.a003", "note": "The article is rendered “the.”", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.003"], "source_head": "WLC.ISA.15.8.003", "status": "aligned", "target": ["t002"], "target_head": "t002"}, {"id": "ISA.15.8.a004", "note": "Nominal correspondence.", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.004"], "source_head": "WLC.ISA.15.8.004", "status": "aligned", "target": ["t003"], "target_head": "t003"}, {"id": "ISA.15.8.a005", "note": "The direct-object marker has no independent English realization because “round about” is retained whole as the verb’s rendering; “gone round the borders” also shows that “about” is not required solely to attach the object.", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.005"], "source_head": "WLC.ISA.15.8.005", "status": "unrepresented", "target": [], "target_head": null}, {"id": "ISA.15.8.a006", "note": "The supplied definite article belongs to the construct noun “borders.”", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.006"], "source_head": "WLC.ISA.15.8.006", "status": "aligned", "target": ["t008", "t009"], "target_head": "t009"}, {"id": "ISA.15.8.a007", "note": "The construct relation is rendered “of Moab.”", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.007"], "source_head": "WLC.ISA.15.8.007", "status": "aligned", "target": ["t010", "t011"], "target_head": "t011"}, {"id": "ISA.15.8.a008", "note": "The extent preposition is rendered “unto.”", "source": ["WLC.ISA.15.8.008